<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!-- CONCEPTS-REF 17.11a.1's last bullet, PROVED rather than asserted.

       That bullet said PdfExporter and HtmlSvgExporter ignore Rotation "for
       shapes exactly as much as for text". Half of that was wrong, and the half
       that was right is what this tool now pins:

         - STROKED SHAPES were never square. InkSurface.FlattenShape has turned
           every point list about ShapeCenter since the vector exporter's first
           commit, so a rotated rectangle reaches the emitter as four corners
           that are already where they belong. Grepping the exporter for
           "Rotation" finds nothing because there is nothing for it to do.
         - TEXT AND IMAGES could not be flattened that way - glyphs and pixels
           are placed by a matrix, not by their corners - so both really did
           export square, and both are fixed together.

       Two claims come out of that, and both have the same failure mode: right
       in the eye, wrong in the arithmetic.

         1. A TURN IS A TURN, NOT A NEAR-TURN. World y runs down and PDF y runs
            up, so the matrix is the world one with its angle negated - a sign
            that reads as correct either way in a code review and is only
            settled by recovering the angle back out of the emitted bytes.
         2. ONE BOX IS SEVERAL RECORDS. A text box is flattened per wrapped
            line, and every line has to turn about the box's ONE centre. Give
            each line its own and the box fans open instead of turning - which
            still looks like "text rotates" in a screenshot. The rigidity check
            below is written so that it FAILS on the fanned version, and that
            failure is demonstrated rather than trusted.

       Nothing below is a copy: the emitters are <Compile Include>d straight out
       of src/Quill, so this measures the REAL PDF bytes and the REAL SVG
       markup. Written to the pattern tools/VeilRoundTrip set for 16.7 and
       tools/LayerRoundTrip and tools/TextRotRoundTrip followed after it.

       No isolation block, and that is deliberate: unlike its three siblings this
       tool never touches LibraryStore, so it has no library to be isolated from.
       It builds vector pages in memory and reads back what the emitters wrote.

       net8.0-windows10.0.19041.0 to match its siblings. Nothing here needs a
       window - the two emitters and FontSubsetter are plain BCL, which is the
       reason the export layer can be measured at all. -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0-windows10.0.19041.0</TargetFramework>
    <Platforms>x64</Platforms>
    <RuntimeIdentifier>win-x64</RuntimeIdentifier>
    <SelfContained>false</SelfContained>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <RootNamespace>Quill.Tools</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>ExportRotRoundTrip</AssemblyName>
    <EnableDefaultCompileItems>false</EnableDefaultCompileItems>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="Program.cs" />
    <!-- The two emitters under test, and the font resolver PdfExporter calls. -->
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Quill\Services\PdfExporter.cs" Link="PdfExporter.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Quill\Services\HtmlSvgExporter.cs" Link="HtmlSvgExporter.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Quill\Services\FontSubsetter.cs" Link="FontSubsetter.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
